Different Perspectives On Globalisation
Globalisation has sparked significant debate among scholars, policymakers, and thinkers. There is
significant disagreement about how and the extent to which globalisation influences economies,
politics and the nation-state. There are three key perspectives on globalisation, with realists and
liberals aligned with different perspectives.
The Three Key Perspectives On Globalisation
Hyperglobalisers
Hyperglobalisers argue that globalisation is a transformative force reshaping the world into a
highly interconnected global system, where nation-states are less relevant and global cooperation
is central to tackling political and economic challenges.
● They believe that technological advancements, the expansion of global markets, and the
growing influence of MNCs are leading to the decline of nation-states as the dominant
players in the global order.
○ In their view, economic globalisation is eroding traditional borders and creating a
global marketplace where capital, goods, and information flow freely across regions.
● Hyperglobalisers believe that global institutions are increasingly important as
nation-states lose their control over economic and political processes. They see global
governance as necessary to manage the interconnected world, tackling issues that
transcend national boundaries, such as climate change and global financial instability.
● Many hyperglobalisers believe in the benefits of this change. They argue that globalisation
can lift millions out of poverty, spread democracy, and facilitate the exchange of knowledge
and technology, leading to overall progress.
Case Study - The European Union:
The European Union (EU) is often cited by hyperglobalisers as an example of how globalisation
has transformed economies and politics. Through the integration of markets, free movement of
goods, capital, and people, and shared political institutions, the EU has reduced the relevance of
individual nation-states in many areas. Supranational institutions like the European Central Bank
and the European Commission manage economic and political processes, allowing member states
to cooperate on issues like climate change and financial regulation, supporting hyperglobalisers’
belief in the benefits of global governance.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 3
Globalisation Sceptics
Globalisation sceptics argue that globalisation has exacerbated divisions between countries and
failed to create a more cooperative, interconnected world. They believe that the decline of the
nation-state is overstated and that countries continue to prioritise their sovereignty and
self-interest over global cooperation.
● They argue that the concept of globalisation is not new or transformative, but rather a
continuation of long-standing patterns of international trade and interaction that have been
present for centuries.
○ As a result, they push back against hyperglobalisers’ assertions that globalisation
has fundamentally changed the nature of global politics.
○ Politics is influenced by other countries, but what happens within countries
continues to be the most important and dominate.
● Globalisation Sceptics believe that the nation-state remains the key actor in global
politics, with intergovernmental institutions like the UN and WTO having little power.
Instead, they simply serve as tools for powerful countries to advance their own interests
rather than fostering genuine global cooperation.
○ Nation-states continue to protect their sovereignty through trade barriers and
immigration controls, with borders remaining highly relevant and little genuine power
being lost by countries.
Case Study - US China Trade War:
The US-China trade war from July 2018 (and again in 2025) supports sceptics’ argument that
nation-states remain the key actors in global politics and that international institutions have little
power to control them. Despite decades of economic integration, China and the US engaged in a
trade conflict that resulted in tariffs and restrictions, undermining the idea that globalisation
inherently leads to harmony and that international institutions are more important than
nation-states. The conflict reflected concerns in both countries about protecting domestic industries
and maintaining sovereignty, reinforcing sceptics’ view that states continue to prioritise their own
interests over global cooperation.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 4
Transformationalists
Transformationalists take a middle-ground perspective. They view globalisation as a powerful
force that is reshaping societies and economies but don’t believe that it is making the nation-state
irrelevant. Rather than fully eroding the power of the nation-state, they argue that globalisation is
transforming global politics and economics but states can adapt and maintain sovereignty.
● They view globalisation as a highly complex, uneven process that varies across regions
and sectors. It doesn’t simply lead to nation-states losing power, instead it influences
different states unevenly. While some states may see their sovereignty reduced, others may
adapt and thrive in the interconnected world.
○ In particular, strong states with large economies such as the US and China can use
the interconnected nature of economies and international institutions as ways to
exert influence.
● Transformationalists argue that globalisation is transforming the role of states, rather than
simply eroding state power. States must adapt to new realities by collaborating with and
working through non-state actors including MNCs, IGOs and NGOs.
○ This collaboration is necessary to address transnational challenges like climate
change, trade regulation, and global security.
Case Study - China and Globalisation:
China’s trajectory over the past few decades supports the transformationalist view of globalisation,
as it has adapted to the global economy while maintaining strong state control. By integrating into
global trade networks, especially after joining the World Trade Organization (WTO) in 2001, China
has experienced rapid economic growth. However, it has not seen a decline in state power;
instead, China has used globalisation to its advantage, strategically collaborating with multinational
corporations and global institutions while asserting its own sovereignty and leveraging its influence
over other states, as shown by the Belt and Road Initiative.
How Liberals and Realists Fit Into These Perspectives
Liberals
● Liberals generally align with the hyperglobalisers’ perspective, seeing globalisation as a
positive force that fosters greater interconnectedness, cooperation, and mutual prosperity.
○ They argue that economic interdependence reduces the likelihood of conflict, as
countries benefit more from trade and cooperation than from competition or war.
○ They also see globalisation as important to economic growth and spreading liberal
values across borders.
● Liberals also view international institutions and cooperation as essential to managing global
challenges, promoting democracy, and protecting human rights.
○ This being said, many liberals wouldn’t go as far as agreeing that globalisation
makes the nation-state irrelevant and that it will be replaced by a world government.
In this sense, they may find themselves between the perspectives of the
hyperglobalisers and transformationalists.
Realists
● Realists align more closely with globalisation sceptics. They argue that despite economic
interconnectedness, the nation-state remains sovereign and remains the most important
actor in global politics, with globalisation doing little to alter this fundamental reality.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 5
○ Realists emphasise that states continue to prioritise national interests and
sovereignty over international cooperation.
● They argue that states often use intergovernmental institutions as tools to pursue their own
goals and influence other states, therefore also showing some alignment with the
perspective of transformationalists.
The Impact Of Globalisation On State Sovereignty
Arguments That Globalisation Has Undermined State Sovereignty
Economic Globalisation Has Removed State Control Over National Economies
● The rise of MNCs, which operate across multiple borders, allow private entities such as
Apple and Amazon to hold substantial economic power, often exceeding that of the states.
○ As they provide significant jobs and taxes to countries yet can threaten to move
production elsewhere, MNCs can influence domestic policy in their interests,
therefore reducing government control over their state’s economy.
○ An example of MNCs influencing domestic policy can be seen in Ireland’s
relationship with Apple, where the company set up its European headquarters in
exchange for favourable tax arrangements. Apple benefited from Ireland’s 12.5%
corporate tax rate, significantly lower than many other countries, and negotiated
additional tax breaks, resulting in an effective tax rate as low as 0.005% in some
years.
■ This arrangement led to a legal battle with the European Commission, which
ordered Ireland to recover €13 billion in unpaid taxes, demonstrating the
power MNCs wield over national economic policy.
○ Another example is the influence of multinational car companies on UK policy.In
2025, the UK Government made a number of changes that benefitted multinational
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 6
car companies including Volswagen and Stellantis, as they were concerned that the
MNCs may stop manufacturing in the UK otherwise and lose thousands of British
jobs as a result.
■ In particular, the government relaxed the Zero Emission Vehicle Mandate, so
that car manufacturers were no longer forced to ensure that a high
percentage of the vehicles they made in the UK were electric.
■ This demonstrates how multinational corporations can have a significant
impact on national policy due to their economic strength, demonstrating that
the control/sovereignty that governments have over their economies is
limited.
● Additionally, a state’s economic performance is now very dependent on global
markets outside of their control, as national economies are increasingly vulnerable to
global fluctuations in trade, finance, and demand.
○ For instance, the 2008 financial crisis, originating in the U.S., had far-reaching
consequences for economies worldwide, demonstrating how interconnected
markets can undermine a state’s internal sovereignty.
○ This reliance on global markets also weakens the state’s ability to set independent
economic policies, as countries must now cater to international investors and
foreign market forces.
● Cultural and political globalisation amplify these challenges to state sovereignty by
spreading consumerism and free market capitalism, which further erode traditional
economic structures and undermine national control over the economy, making states
struggle to maintain both political and economic control.
○ For example, the IMF and World Bank have frequently made vital economic aid
contingent on countries (often in the global south) moving towards free-market
economies.
Global Issues Force Cooperation and Undermine States’ Ability to Act Unilaterally
● As globalisation has made states increasingly interlinked, a large amount of the
challenges that face states today transcend national borders and require states to
cooperate through international institutions to tackle them, rather than being able to act
unilaterally.
○ Climate change has led to agreements like the Paris Agreement, where countries
seek to collectively tackle the problem by reducing emissions.
○ Similarly, the COVID-19 pandemic highlighted the need for global collaboration in
vaccine distribution, travel restrictions, and economic recovery plans.
■ Due to global travel and the interconnected nature of economies, a country’s
individual efforts at tackling the pandemic could be undermined by other
countries.
● Economic globalisation intensifies this need for political cooperation, as
interconnected economies rely on shared financial regulations to prevent and respond to
crises like the 2008 financial crisis, which required global coordination through institutions
such as the International Monetary Fund (IMF) and G20.
● This interconnectedness pressures states to surrender aspects of sovereignty, shifting
decision-making power to international bodies like the World Health Organisation (WHO) or
the UN, which enforce collective decisions that can override unilateral national action.
○ As a result, national sovereignty becomes increasingly subordinated to global
governance frameworks.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 7
Political Globalisation Constrains How States Can Act
● Political globalisation has created international frameworks that limit state autonomy, as
IGOs like the UN, ICC and EU set binding rules on issues such as trade, human rights, and
conflict resolution. If states break these rules, their sovereignty can be justifiably
infringed upon in the name of upholding the rules.
○ In 2015, Greece was sanctioned by the EU for breaching the Common Agricultural
Policy (CAP) by providing unlawful subsidies to its farmers. The EU required Greece
to repay €425 million in misused funds, demonstrating how supranational
institutions can infringe upon a state's internal sovereignty.
○ In 2011, the UN sanctioned a military intervention in Libya under UN Security
Council Resolution 1973 to protect civilians from human rights abuses by the
Gaddafi regime during the Libyan Civil War. The resolution authorised a no-fly zone
and “all necessary measures” to protect civilians, leading to an international military
campaign primarily conducted by NATO forces.
■ This intervention marked a case where the UN sanctioned the use of force,
overriding Libya’s sovereignty to uphold international humanitarian norms
and prevent further atrocities against civilians.
● International law therefore challenges state sovereignty by requiring compliance with
global norms, limiting the freedom of states to act independently in pursuit of their national
interests.
Arguments That Globalisation Hasn’t Undermined State Sovereignty
Economic Globalisation Hasn’t Undermined States’ Sovereignty
● While economic globalisation has increased economic interdependence, it can be argued
that states retain a significant amount of control over their national economies, using
policy tools like tariffs, regulations, and subsidies to protect their national interests.
○ China, for instance, participates in global markets but maintains firm control over
critical economic sectors such as technology and state-owned enterprises. This
control allows China to shape its economic development according to national
priorities while benefiting from international trade.
○ The United States has also demonstrated that it can impose tariffs and protectionist
measures, as seen during the US-China trade war, where the U.S. sought to shield
its manufacturing sector from competition.
○ Many countries including Japan and India provide agricultural subsidies to support
domestic food production and protect key national interests, ensuring sovereignty
over key sectors even in a globalised economy.
● These examples show that while states engage in global trade, they retain the power
to regulate and protect their economies.
States Still Have Internal Sovereignty
Despite globalisation, states maintain the ability to control borders, regulate immigration, and
protect national interests in global negotiations. The rise of populism across the world has led
to many states reasserting their sovereignty.
● Australia provides a clear example of how countries maintain sovereignty through strict
border and immigration policies. The country’s ‘Operation Sovereign Borders’ aims to
prevent unauthorised illegal immigration by using the Australian Defence Force (ADF) and
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 8
border protection agencies to intercept boats carrying asylum seekers and process asylum
claims in offshore facilities.
○ This demonstrates that globalisation has not eroded a state’s capacity to regulate
migration and protect its territorial integrity.
● Additionally, in international negotiations, states can assert their national interests and
achieve favourable terms.
○ During the renegotiation of NAFTA into the United States-Mexico-Canada
Agreement (USMCA) in 2018, Canada successfully protected its dairy industry by
negotiating limits on U.S. dairy imports, asserting national interest in preserving the
domestic market for its dairy farmers.
States Choose To Participate in Political Globalisation and Can Opt Out
● States voluntarily choose to participate in political globalisation by joining international
organisations and institutions, but they retain the ability to leave or not join.
○ The UK’s exit from the European Union through Brexit shows that countries can
withdraw from supranational institutions to reclaim control over domestic policies
such as trade, immigration, and legal frameworks.
● Similarly, not all states have ratified the International Criminal Court (ICC) treaty,
with major powers like the United States, Russia, and China opting not to join,
reflecting their unwillingness to cede sovereignty in matters of international justice.
● Additionally, states often have the power to ignore rulings or recommendations from
international bodies.
○ In 2016, China rejected a ruling by the Permanent Court of Arbitration that
invalidated its territorial claims in the South China Sea, continuing to assert control
over the region despite the international tribunal’s decision, demonstrating its
willingness to defy global rulings in favour of national interests.
○ This reflects a realist perspective that international organisations hold limited real
power over sovereign nations. This ability to opt in or out of global governance
frameworks underscores the idea that states remain the ultimate decision-makers in
the global system.
