A* Level Perspective - Globalisation Has Undermined The Sovereignty Of
Some States More Than Others
It can be argued that globalisation has undermined the sovereignty of some states more than
others. Wealthier and more powerful states retain significant sovereignty and influence within
global politics. In comparison, poorer and less powerful states have less power to project their
interests and are more constrained by economic and political globalisation.
● Powerful states can use their economic power to shape global rules and bend
globalisation to their advantage, maintaining control over domestic policy and forcing
other countries to follow global rules that primarily benefit wealthier countries.
○ For example, the US exerted influence in the WTO through its successful push for
intellectual property protections under the Agreement on Trade-Related Aspects of
Intellectual Property Rights (TRIPS).
■ The U.S. lobbied for stronger global intellectual property protections,
particularly benefiting American pharmaceutical and technology companies.
■ This allowed the U.S. to secure favorable rules that protect patents and
copyrights, ensuring that its industries maintain a competitive advantage in
global markets, especially in sectors like pharmaceuticals, where patent
protections are crucial for profitability.
● Importantly, powerful states can also decide to ignore international law and rulings when it
is convenient. They know they won’t be punished/harmed in the same way weaker states
would.
○ For example, the US invaded Iraq in 2003 to pursue their own interests despite it
not being authorised by the UN and being illegal under international law.
● In contrast, weaker states’ sovereignty is more compromised due to their
dependence on foreign aid and global institutions.
○ Greece during the Eurozone crisis had to accept austerity measures and structural
reforms mandated by the IMF and European Central Bank (ECB), limiting its ability
to enact independent economic policies.
■ These reforms led to great pain for many in the economy, but Greece was
forced to accept the measures as they required economic assistance.
○ Similarly, many African and Latin American nations, reliant on loans from the World
Bank or foreign investment, have faced pressure to implement free-market reforms
or deregulation, reducing their ability to protect domestic industries or set
independent economic policies.
■ These reforms are often in the interests of more powerful states which seek
to maintain economic access.
● Further, weaker states can’t ignore international law and rulings in the same way that
powerful states can. They are the subject of the majority of international rulings.
○ The ICC has disproportionately focused on Africa, with the vast majority of cases
being from African nations, despite human rights violations occurring globally.
○ When Iraq invaded Kuwait in 1990, the UN authorised the use of military force to
intervene. This led to Operation Desert Storm in 1991, where a U.S.-led coalition
successfully pushed Iraqi forces out of Kuwait.
■ There was no such similar military intervention when the US invaded Iraq in
2003.
