The Advantages And Disadvantages Of Globalisation
Economic Globalisation
For more detail and examples on this, look at the Globalisation and Contemporary Issues video on
the Politics Explained YouTube Channel and the accompanying section of the Global Politics
Updated Textbook on the Politics Explained website.
The Advantages Of Economic Globalisation
It can be argued that economic globalisation has had a profound positive impact on global
economies by promoting growth, fostering innovation, and reducing poverty.
● One of the key benefits has been the expansion of international trade, allowing countries to
access larger markets and thus increase economic output and job creation.
○ Between 2000 and 2024, the global economy has nearly tripled in size, with nominal
global GDP increasing from about $33 trillion in 2000 to approximately $104 trillion
by 2023, according to data from the World Bank and IMF.
○ This has particularly benefited emerging economies like China, which experienced
rapid industrialisation and saw over 800 million people lifted out of poverty in the
past few decades.
● Globalisation has also led to foreign direct investment (FDI), bringing much-needed capital
into developing countries, boosting infrastructure and creating employment opportunities.
● The free flow of technology and knowledge has also helped developing nations access
advanced technologies, enabling them to leapfrog traditional stages of industrialisation and
improve productivity.
○ For example, renewable energy technologies have spread rapidly across borders,
helping countries reduce their reliance on fossil fuels.
● Beyond economic growth, globalisation has increased consumer access to a wider range of
goods and services, driving down prices and improving living standards worldwide.
Furthermore, the increased economic interconnectedness between nations has fostered
international cooperation, contributing to a more stable global economy.
The Disadvantages Of Economic Globalisation
Despite its promises, economic globalisation has not had uniformly positive impacts, especially for
developing nations and vulnerable populations.
● One major criticism is that it has deepened global inequality, with wealthier nations and
multinational corporations benefiting disproportionately while poorer countries remain
reliant on exporting raw materials and low-wage labor.
○ The rise of global supply chains has led to a “race to the bottom,” where countries
compete by offering cheap labor, leading to poor working conditions and limited
worker protections, particularly in industries like textiles and electronics.
○ IGOs such as the IMF and World Bank often make financial assistance conditional
on countries implementing free market reforms, which benefit wealthier countries by
giving them continued economic access to raw materials but have in many cases
deepened poverty and prevented economic development.
● Economic globalisation has also left many countries vulnerable to global market
fluctuations, as seen during the 2008 financial crisis, where interconnected financial
markets collapsed, triggering economic downturns worldwide, disproportionately affecting
smaller economies such as Ukraine and Jamaica.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 11
● Additionally, globalisation has enabled the dominance of MNCs, which often exploit weaker
states by pressuring governments to enact favourable regulations or they will leave the
country and move jobs away.
○ This erosion of state sovereignty, combined with the concentration of wealth and
power in fewer hands, has worsened inequality and left many countries dependent
on external forces, limiting their ability to independently shape their economic
futures.
● Finally, the rapid industrialisation and expansion of global trade have increased carbon
emissions, deforestation, and resource exploitation, contributing to climate change and
environmental degradation.
Political Globalisation
Many of the points below are covered in more detail in other videos on the Politics Explained
YouTube Channel and the Global Politics Updated Textbook on the Politics Explained website.
The Advantages Of Political Globalisation
Political globalisation has had a profound positive impact by fostering international cooperation,
enhancing global governance, and promoting peace and stability.
● One of the key benefits has been the establishment of IGOs like the UN and WHO, which
facilitate collaboration on global issues such as conflict resolution, human rights, public
health, and climate change.
○ For example, the Paris Agreement on climate change brought together nearly 200
countries to commit to reducing carbon emissions and tackling global warming.
● Similarly, political globalisation has helped in the promotion of human rights through the UN
and ICC in particular, holding individuals accountable for crimes against humanity and
sanctioning intervention to prevent abuses.
○ Political globalisation also plays a critical role in conflict resolution, as seen in UN
peacekeeping missions and diplomatic efforts that prevent conflicts from escalating.
● This growing interconnectedness among nations through political globalisation has
contributed to a more stable and cooperative global system.
The Disadvantages Of Political Globalisation
Despite its intentions, political globalisation has not always had positive outcomes and has often
been criticised for undermining national sovereignty and failing to achieve its goals.
● International organisations like the UN and IMF have been accused of imposing policies
that primarily benefit powerful nations, leaving weaker states with little influence.
○ For example, IMF-led reforms often require states to implement austerity measures
that can worsen poverty and inequality, particularly in developing countries.
● Additionally, institutions like the EU and WTO have been criticised for eroding state
sovereignty, forcing nations to comply with rules that do not necessarily align with their
national interests.
○ This dynamic has also created tensions, as seen in the Brexit referendum, where
concerns over loss of control motivated the UK’s decision to leave the EU.
● Finally, realists would argue that IGOs in fact have little power to influence states and
uphold peace or human rights, as shown in Israel’s war in Gaza and Russia’s invasion of
Ukraine, both of which have violated international law in different ways.
