Cultural Globalisation
The Advantages Of Cultural Globalisation
It can be argued that cultural globalisation has had a positive impact by facilitating the exchange of
ideas, values, and traditions across borders, fostering understanding and global unity.
● One key benefit is the promotion of cultural diversity, where different cuisines, fashion, art,
and music have spread globally. This has allowed people to experience and appreciate
cultures beyond their own, breaking down cultural barriers.
○ For example, K-pop from South Korea has gained immense global popularity,
connecting different cultures through entertainment, whilst Hollywood and Bollywood
stars are known across the world.
● Furthermore, the widespread use of social media platforms like Instagram and YouTube
has accelerated the spread of social and political movements, including those which
advocate for social justice and environmental awareness.
○ Fridays for Future, started by Greta Thunberg, which united young people
worldwide to address climate change and pressured governments to put climate
change to the front of the political agenda.
○ Similarly, the #MeToo movement gained traction internationally and sparked
conversations about gender equality and sexual harassment across cultures.
● It can also be argued that the spread of English as a global language has improved
communication and collaboration across nations, aiding education and business.
○ Globalisation has enriched education by enabling cross-cultural exchanges through
programs like Erasmus, allowing students to study abroad and experience diverse
cultures firsthand.
● Finally, cultural globalisation has also been instrumental in spreading values like
democracy, human rights, and environmental sustainability, seen through initiatives like the
United Nations’ Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs).
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 13
The Disadvantages Of Cultural Globalisation
Cultural globalisation has been criticised for promoting homogenisation and eroding local cultures,
leading to what some refer to as a global monoculture.
● As MNCs and media conglomerates dominate global markets, local traditions, languages,
and customs are often overshadowed by more dominant global cultures.
○ The spread of fast food chains like McDonald’s and Hollywood movies has led to a
dilution of traditional culinary practices and local entertainment industries.
○ Further, as global media conglomerates have gained influence, local artists and
cultural creators have lost some control over how their culture is represented.
Cultural narratives and traditions are increasingly shaped by global media
corporations rather than by local communities themselves, resulting in
misrepresentation and cultural dilution.
■ For example, Disney films Pocahontas and Moana have been criticised for
misrepresenting indigenous cultures, commercialising their traditions for
global audiences while oversimplifying their histories and beliefs.
■ Similarly, African cultural symbols and art are often commodified and sold in
global markets by non-African companies, stripping these works of their
deeper cultural significance and exploiting them for profit.
● Critics argue that cultural globalisation is essentially a form of Americanisation, as
American culture, through music, movies, and consumer goods, has disproportionately
shaped global cultural norms.
○ This dominance of Western, particularly American, values can lead to the
marginalisation of indigenous cultures and contribute to the loss of cultural diversity.
● Finally, cultural globalisation has contributed to the decline of less commonly spoken
languages, as global languages like English dominate international business, media, and
education. This leads to the gradual extinction of indigenous and minority languages,
weakening cultural heritage.
○ Despite revitalisation efforts, the Maori language has struggled due to the
dominance of English in media, education, and public life, leading to a generational
gap in fluent speakers in New Zealand.
